{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And He said further.</b> Another name, and it is for the same reason as the first, except that the one [Ehyeh] is in the language of the speaker [first person], while this [YHWH] is singular language that is not [first-person] speech, and is derived from יה. And these three names are proper nouns. Now I shall explain to you the reason of the proper noun, and the reason of these letters, and part of the secret of the honored Name, and why it is not pronounced as written." "Said Abraham the author. Now I have consented to elaborate, for I need a laid foundation. Know that a proper noun is one set as a sign and symbol to the one who says it and the one who hears it, of the essence of the thing named. And a proper noun is distinguished from an adjective in four ways. First: an adjective is derived from a verb-form, like חָכַם — \"if your heart is wise\" (Proverbs 23:15) [is] past tense, \"that you may become wise\" (Proverbs 19:20) [is] future tense — but not so a proper noun, like Abraham: past or future cannot be derived from it. Even though there is an essence derived from a verb-form, like Isaac, one does not say יצחקתי or אצחק, for a proper noun has no inflection. The second matter: you pluralize the adjective חכם and say חכמים, but this is impossible with Abraham, which is a proper noun — one does not pluralize it as אברהמים, nor with Israel, which is a proper noun: if it were the name of two men, one would not say ישראלים; rather, that form appears only when one relates a man to Israel, which is a generic noun. The third matter: a proper noun is not determined with the definite article as an adjective is — from חכם one says \"the wise man\" (Ecclesiastes 2:14), but one does not say האברהם or היצחק. And there is no objection from the word \"said the Qohelet\" (Ecclesiastes 12:8), for it is not a proper noun but an adjective for the wisdom gathered in him. And האדם is an adjective and has a secret, for it is a generic noun; and the word המנשה (Deuteronomy 3:13) is because of genealogical relation. The fourth matter: a proper noun does not enter construct state, as an adjective does, like חכם לבב (Job 9:4) — one does not say יצחק הדור, for the proper noun stands on its own. Now thus the word אהיה, as well as the honored four-letter Name, since both are proper nouns. Now we have found ד׳ צבאות (Isaiah 1:9), and many were compelled to say that צבאות is a proper noun, or that it is a sign for His host. But this is impossible, for behold: \"the God of the hosts\" (Amos 3:13), and it never appears alone but only with Elohim, or with the honored Name — יהוה Elohim. And let it not seem difficult to you \"YHWH Elohim of hosts\" (Psalms 59:6), for it is like \"and the prophecy of Oded the prophet\" (2 Chronicles 15:5) — because the Name who dwells forever stands alone, and in Him all stands; therefore sometimes this Name functions like an adjective, in the manner of \"and He remembered the days of old, Moses His people\" (Isaiah 63:11) — the meaning being that He is the one who makes [them] stand. Therefore YHWH Elohim are joined with Him, the holy angels, and thus ד׳ צבאות — they are the hosts of heaven. And do not be willing to hear the words of the Gaon [Saadia], who says that He is called thus because of Israel, and he brought proof from \"and I will bring out My hosts\" (Exodus 7:4) — do you not see \"and all the host of heaven standing by Him\" (1 Kings 22:19)?" "Now Elohim is an adjective of the Name, for it is in plural and singular form like \"Is not El who is high in the heavens\" (Job 22:12), and furthermore it enters construct state: \"Elohim, the God of Israel\" (Psalms 72:18). And the word אֵל is strong, and thus Shaddai — \"like the sound of many waters, like the sound of Shaddai\" (Ezekiel 1:24), and \"Shaddai will be your treasures\" (Job 22:25)." "And know that one is the secret of all number and its foundation, and two is the beginning of the number of evens, and three is the beginning of the number of odds. Now all numbers are nine in one manner and ten in another. If you write the nine in a circle and fold the end against each number, you will find the units on the left, and the tens resembling the units toward the right side, and when you reach five, which is the middle, the numbers reverse, the tens becoming units and the units tens. And in another manner they are ten sefirot, without nothingness, for you cannot begin with one unless there are ten. Now ten resembles one, and it is a name encompassing the units, which are from one to ten, and the name ten encompasses them while being itself the first number. And the beginning of the numbers resembling the units: when you reach twenty, they are two tens corresponding to two units, and thirty corresponding to three units, and so all tens up to ninety — they are nine tens corresponding to nine units. And when you reach the number one hundred, it resembles one; and when you reach nine hundred, they too correspond to nine units, until you reach a thousand, which is ten hundreds — that too, the thousand, resembles one, until they are nine thousands corresponding to nine units. And when you reach ten thousand, the reckoning is complete, being one רבבה, and likewise up to ten רבבות in this manner. For all the heads of the numbers resemble one; therefore the masters of number said that all numbers are a part of ten, or generated from its doubling, or from combining it with its units, or from the two ways combined. Now the ten sefirot are as the number of ten fingers, five opposite five, and thus the spheres are nine, which are noble bodies that stand, and the tenth, which is קדש, is called thus because its force is in the entire Throne of Glory, and it is the powerful one encompassing all bodies. Now the middles in the number ten are two, and they are five, and they are six, and these four — אהו״י — are the honored ones. And every square number where one is added to the square — thus is it in its root and thus in its analogue, and so in the square of five by five, and in the square of six by six — these four numbers always preserve themselves as squares, and this is their superiority over all other square numbers. Furthermore, every number exists in one in potentiality, and it exists in every number in actuality, and it does with one aspect what every number does with two. Now the power of one: when you join its square to the square of its double, they become five, and this is equal reckoning in squares — for if you join its square to the square of its double, it will be the cube of five. And every number before five will have the ratio of its cube to the two squares equal to the ratio of the reckoning to five, and above five the matter reverses. And the reckoning of six is a perfect number in its parts, and there is no perfect number in every order except one. And when you join the square of one to the square of the head of the odds, the sum is its analogue. And if you draw a circular diagonal in its number and extract a third as remainder, it will be the equilateral triangle, whose two equal sides equal the number of the enclosing line, and likewise the oblong square in a circle, and before this number the ratio of the triangle to the line is as its ratio to ten, and above it the reverse." "Now these four numbers are the precious ones, and their total is twenty-two, as the number of all the letters. And do not count the doubled letters, for they are themselves — only what was in width was returned in length. And the reason is that in pronunciation, in the language [in which] they have one name, they resemble one another, but they do not resemble one another in writing. And a master of the holy tongue observed that there are five places of articulation for the letters. Now half [the letters] are always roots, and half are sometimes roots and sometimes additions for a reason at the beginning of a word or at its end. Now this half is more honored than the first, and it is fitting that in every place of articulation the light letter, easy to pronounce, be chosen to express many [sounds], and it is fitting that the first letter, also the fifth, also the sixth, also the tenth, be light in pronunciation and heavy in meaning, and they will be found with the vowels so that they are four letters of continuance. And they will sometimes be visible and sometimes hidden, sometimes added and sometimes deficient, sometimes absorbed in dagesh, and sometimes interchanged with one another." "Now the guttural letters are four, and their beginning is ע, then ח. And do not consider the letter as you would pronounce it with the aforementioned additions together with the letter, for they are not guttural letters; rather, the letter alone, when you place a patach beneath it. And of these two, ע and ח are very heavy, and one who did not practice in his youth to pronounce them will not be able to articulate them, like the Gentile. Therefore the alef and the he were chosen. Also from the palatal letters גיכ״ק, which are after the gutturals, the light ones were chosen, and they are י and כ; therefore ג and ק were among the roots." "And the lingual letters דטלנ״ת — of the two that are roots, they are ד and ט, and he chose the others. And the dental letters זסשר״ץ are all heavy, and the lightest among them is ש, therefore he chose it and left the others. Therefore he needed to take from the labial letters בומ״ף three, and he left the one, which is פ." "And it is fitting that the beginning of the letters be from the guttural, for it is the first place of articulation in the structure of man, from bottom to top, and the alef before the he, for it is the lightest of the guttural letters; therefore it was set as head of all the letters, corresponding to one. And it is fitting that the he be the fifth letter, corresponding to five in number, which is honored. And these two letters are lighter in pronunciation than all the letters. Since the first letter is from the guttural, he placed after it a letter from the end of the five places, which is the labial — therefore bet after alef, and vav after he, and pe after ayin. Now he needed to place two more letters until he reached the he, and he placed one from the palatal, which is gimel, and the second from the lingual, which is dalet. Therefore he needed to place zayin, which is from the dentals, after vav. Now he needed to place a fourth letter, which corresponds to the number ten — the tenth of the letters. He had already taken from the guttural the two honored and manifest ones, and from the labial the light and honored one, which is the vav. And among their places of articulation there is no root-letter lighter than those of the palate, and the chosen ones were yod and kaf, and yod is lighter." "Now four letters are fit to be letters of continuance, and because the alef is like the one, it is therefore found hidden with every vowel: with cholam — רֹאשׁ; with kamatz gadol — רָאשִׁים; with chirik — רֵאשִׁית; with kamatz katan — רֵאשִׁית; with shurek — פֻארָה (Isaiah 10:33); with patach gadol — פֶלֶא (Exodus 15:11). Because it is a head, they made it a sign of the speaker, masculine and feminine. And because it is never found visible at the end, it is therefore not a sign [there]; only sometimes it is interchanged with he — קראן לי מרא (Ruth 1:20), גבהא קומתו (Ezekiel 31:5). And it is found added at the end, like ואתיקיהא (Ezekiel 41:15), ההלכוא אתו (Joshua 10:24). It also appears at the beginning, added — אתמול (2 Samuel 5:2), אזרועך (Job 31:22) — and in the middle — והאזניחו נהרות (Isaiah 19:6). And it may be written at the end and not pronounced, like חטא (Deuteronomy 23:22). And sometimes it is deficient, like על כס יה (Exodus 17:16). And it is interchanged with he — האמון (Jeremiah 52:15) / ההמון (2 Samuel 18:29); also with yod — תתימרו (Isaiah 61:6) / יתאמרו (Psalms 94:4); also with vav in pronunciation — לעמשה תמרו (2 Samuel 19:14)." "Now three letters are fit to be signs at the beginning and at the end. The letter yod, which resembles one — they placed it at the end as a sign of the speaker, masculine and feminine, in verbs: שמרתי (Psalms 17:4), אמרתי (Exodus 23:13), and in nouns like שמי (Exodus 15:2). And they placed it at the beginning as a sign of the singular masculine — it resembles what exists, just as the number ten resembles one. And they placed it at the end, the opposite of this, as a sign corresponding to the feminine: דְעִי (1 Samuel 25:17), רְאִי (ibid.), למען תִּזְכְּרִי (Ezekiel 16:63). And because in number it resembles one, it serves for relation; and because ten encompasses all reckoning, therefore the yod at the end encompasses a sign of plural in construct forms: יְדֵי נשים (Lamentations 4:10), and in construct to the speaker: יָדַי נטו שמים (Isaiah 45:12). And the yod is found hidden with chirik — רַגְלִי (Psalms 26:12); with kamatz katan — רגלי (1 Samuel 2:9); with kamatz gadol — יָדָיו, רַגְלָיו. And the yod in place of he — יד יהוה הוֹיָה (Exodus 9:3), אתה הוה להם למלך (Nehemiah 6:6); in place of he — יֶחֱסָיון (Psalms 36:5) [from] חסה; in place of alef — כל פקודי כל ישרתי (Psalms 119:128). And it is found added — יצהר (Numbers 11:8), היושבי (Psalms 123:1), רבתי (Lamentations 1:1). And the he — they placed it as a sign of existence: \"הא לכם זרע\" (Genesis 4:25) [means] thus; sometimes as a demonstrative — הבמחנים (Numbers 13:19), הנהיה (Deuteronomy 4:32); and sometimes for the determined — האיש, העבד העברי (Genesis 39:17), האנשים (Genesis 19:5). And at the end it is a sign of the feminine in past-tense language, also in nouns — for the masculine they left without a sign: אמר, אמרה, קהלת (Ecclesiastes 12:8). It was also a sign of the feminine in verbs in the futures: אשמרנה, ישמרנה, נשמרנה, תשמרנה, ישמרוה, שמרנוה, שמרוה. And a sign in nouns — נערה, בתולה (Deuteronomy 22:28). And the he is added in the intensive stem and in the reflexive stem, and it is absorbed in this — בידך (Exodus 4:2) — like the yod in בטרם אצרךָ (Jeremiah 1:5), and like the alef in וָאַבֶּדְךָ כרוב הסוכך (Ezekiel 28:16). And it is added at the end — אשמעה (Psalms 86:6), and in nouns like לילה (Isaiah 21:12). And it is hidden with kamatz — עָשָׂה; with kamatz katan — בונֵה ירושלים (Psalms 147:2); with patach katan — אשר אני בונֶה (2 Chronicles 2:4); with cholam — כי פְרָעֹה אהרן (Exodus 32:25), also שְׁלֹמֹה (1 Kings 1:17); with shurek — לא יִקְרְחֻה קרחה (Leviticus 21:5)." "And because the vav's place of articulation is from the joining of the lips, they placed it at the beginning to join one thing with another — אברהם ויצחק (Genesis 48:16), מי פעל ועשה (Isaiah 41:4). And at the end it is the language of plural masculines and also feminines in past tense, and a sign in the futures for masculines and sometimes also for feminines — אם תמצאו (Song of Songs 5:8), אם תעירו (Song of Songs 2:7). And the vav is found hidden with shurek and with cholam. And because the he was added at the end as a sign of the feminine, they needed the vav to be a sign for the masculine — שְמָרוֹ, also in nouns — יָדוֹ, also in futures — אויב יִרְדְפוֹ (Hosea 8:3). And with the addition of he, or nun with dagesh, or shurek — ישמרהו (Psalms 41:3), יִשְמְרֶנּוּ (Exodus 21:36). And it is a sign of plural language for speakers in past tense and in futures." "Now I have shown that these four letters are letters of continuance; therefore the honored names, which are proper nouns, are from these letters. Now the first name [Ehyeh] does not appear in Scripture except in this place. And we know that Moses did not speak to Pharaoh the words of the Name except as written, nor did he mention it by substitute, for it was holy. Therefore the ancients needed to substitute for it. And the meaning of וזה is masculine [demonstrative], like זה שמי, for the meaning is repeated. And the proof is that we call it Elohim when it is joined with א׳ ד׳ [Adonai], the meaning being that He is Lord. And the word came in the manner of plural language as an honorific, like Elohim. Therefore one who says to the Name as one says to a man אַדֹנִי שאל (Genesis 44:19) is mistaken." "And now I shall mention the reason. Know that there are three worlds. The first is the low world, and it has many levels, but three encompass them. One is the inanimates, and they are seven corresponding to the seven servants. Above them are the plants, and they have many levels. Higher than them are the living beings, and their levels are many, and man alone is in the highest level of the low world. And his preciousness is equal to his essence — in his body, also in his soul corresponding to his thought, also in his affairs and all his occupations. And the wise of heart likened the species, which are the universals that are preserved — for the particulars perish — they likened those universal species standing to the shade of a tree over running waters always." "And the middle world has many levels. Now the five planetary stars — their greatness is great, for they stand on their own; they are not extinguished and do not diminish, and their motion does not change, neither increasing nor decreasing, neither rising nor falling, except according to the arrangement. They have many variations: sometimes the star is on the ecliptic line, sometimes to the left, sometimes to the right, much or little; sometimes it rises in its small epicycle also in its large sphere, whose body is distant from the body of the earth; sometimes its sphere rises or descends; it runs and sometimes delays, and sometimes stands, and sometimes turns backward. And all this is relative to the earth — sometimes it is seen and sometimes hidden, sometimes in the east and sometimes in the west. And its relation to its conjunction, and also to the host of heaven above and to the moon below, is always changing beyond reckoning: for sometimes it conjoins with one of the seven [planets] that are visible. Now the conjunctions alone at one level, out of three hundred and sixty, are one hundred and twenty. And because of these variations, all created beings in the low world change in their essence, and even more so in their accidents. But they [the planets] in their essence and in their light do not change. Therefore below them is the moon, for all their accidents afflict it and more, for it has two spheres whose center is not like the center of the earth, and furthermore its motion in the small epicycle is contrary to the motion of the large sphere, and also it has no light of its own but only from the sun. And the proof: when it conjoins with it at the Head or Tail of the Dragon, the sun is not seen if it is day, and if it is opposite the sun at night in one of the aforementioned places, the light of the moon will be absent." "Now above the planets are the stars of the zodiac sphere, and they are fixed in one sphere, not moving like the planets, and their width does not change, nor their relation to one another. One motion they have, without additions or diminutions. Their relation to the earth is constant, and the variation of their appearance is according to the places of the earth and the motion of the sun. And the upper world is the world of the holy angels, who are not bodies and not in bodies, like the soul of man. And their levels are exalted beyond facile conceptions compared to them. And all this world is glory, and all of it stands; and there is no motion of change in its relation, except that its standing is not in itself but in the honored Name alone. And the soul of man is of their species, and it receives supernal power according to the arrangement of the planets. And each planet corresponds to the great host at the time of its creation. And if the soul grows wise, it will stand in the assembly of the angels and be able to receive great power from supernal power that it receives through the light of the angels — then it will be joined to the honored Name. And this is what Jacob vowed: \"and YHWH will be to me for Elohim\" (Genesis 28:21), for he would devote himself all the days of his life to cleave to the Name according to his strength. Therefore he said: \"Remove the foreign gods\" (Genesis 35:2), and until that day he had not said thus, for then he began to serve the Name according to his vow. Therefore Jethro said: \"Now I know that YHWH is greater than all the gods\" (Exodus 18:11), and he was a great sage — and the proof: \"and they asked each man of his fellow\" (Exodus 18:7), and furthermore \"and he prostrated himself\" (Exodus 18:7), and he had no fear of Him." "And because \"man\" saw that he would die, he begot a son so that the species be preserved. Now he received the power of standing in a general way; therefore Eve said: \"I have acquired a man with YHWH\" (Genesis 4:1). And the sons of the prophets would devote themselves in isolation, perhaps each would receive according to his strength. And through this name [YHWH] signs and wonders are renewed in the world. And when this honored Name was spoken to him [Moses], he sought signs and wonders. Therefore you will not find it in the book of Ecclesiastes, for it speaks of the deeds concerning which there is nothing to add and from which there is nothing to subtract, and there is nothing new — for it is supernal wisdom."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :register {:flags {1 ["God"], 2 ["God"], 11 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {1 "Resolved הגאון as the Gaon [Saadia] per standard discipline. The argument about צבאות is left as compressed as the original, including the elliptic appeal to Isaiah 63:11.", 8 "The Hebrew examples are cited from multiple biblical verses; the reference after each form is given parenthetically in the original and retained."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T20:24:30.194851573Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}